# ============================================================================
# DEVELOPMENT-ONLY SPEECH CONFIGURATION — NOT FOR ANY DEPLOYED ENVIRONMENT
# ============================================================================
#
# What this is
# ------------
# The canonical, reviewable record of what "development speech is switched on"
# means for this project. Both settings default to `none` everywhere else, and
# that default is what makes a deployment unable to acquire a speech provider by
# accident. This file is the one place where turning them on is written down.
#
# Why it is a separate file
# -------------------------
# `.env` is git-ignored and holds real secrets, so it cannot be reviewed and
# cannot be cited. This file holds no secret of any kind, so it can be read in a
# diff and pointed at from a test — and `tests/unit/test_development_speech_config.py`
# does exactly that: it parses this file and asserts that the values in it are
# the ones the production guard refuses. If someone edits this file to name a
# provider that production would accept, that test fails.
#
# How to apply it -- and how NOT to
# --------------------------------
# Export these into the environment of the process that runs the server, and
# nothing else:
#
#   ENVIRONMENT=development STT_PROVIDER=faster-whisper TTS_PROVIDER=pyttsx3 #     python -m uvicorn app.main:app --reload
#
# DO NOT append them to `.env`. This was tried and reverted on 2026-10-13, and
# the reason is worth keeping: `pydantic-settings` loads `.env` (see
# `app/core/config.py`) and `tests/conftest.py` pins neither provider, so the
# entire test suite inherits whatever `.env` says. With these three lines in
# `.env` the suite began running real CPU inference and real host-TTS calls
# inside tests written against the `none` default -- it reached 10% in the time
# the whole run normally takes, and the tests covering the "no provider
# configured" path were no longer covering it. A configuration that silently
# rewrites what the test suite tests is worse than no configuration.
#
# Per-process is also the honest scope: these adapters are a property of one
# developer's machine, not of the repository.
#
# Without one of those, a development server answers a committed turn with the
# typed `stt_unavailable` condition rather than a transcript. That is correct
# behaviour, not a bug: nothing in this system is allowed to invent a transcript
# when no recogniser is configured.
#
# What these two adapters are, honestly
# -------------------------------------
# faster-whisper  CPU inference inside the request path. The `tiny.en` weights
#                 are already present on this host from a previous increment; no
#                 download is performed and none is authorised.
# pyttsx3         Drives the *host's* speech engine. On this machine that is
#                 Windows SAPI5. It has never been proven on Linux —
#                 `tests/unit/test_tts_linux_contract.py` skips and says so — so
#                 a successful synthesis here is evidence about this developer's
#                 Windows host and about nothing else.
#
# Neither is approved for production. No production speech provider has been
# approved at all. ElevenLabs remains excluded. Setting either of these with
# ENVIRONMENT=production is a hard startup failure by design — see
# `validate_production_settings` in `app/core/config.py`.
# ============================================================================

ENVIRONMENT=development
STT_PROVIDER=faster-whisper
TTS_PROVIDER=pyttsx3
